## Risk Tiers and Claude Autonomy

This is the most important section. Claude must respect these boundaries without exception.

### Tier 0 — Observational (read-only, no DB2 connection required)
*Examples: log space check, health check metrics queries, explain advisor output*

Claude may: edit freely, suggest and apply changes, run shellcheck locally.
Claude must not: execute against a live database directly.

### Tier 1 — Statistics (read-write to catalog only, online, reversible)
*Examples: runstats.sh, runstats_post_reorg.sh*

Claude may: edit logging, output formatting, Ansible integration, OS-level logic.
Claude must flag for DBA review: any change to the RUNSTATS SQL clause, sampling mode
logic, REORGCHK thresholds (`REORG_OVERFLOW_PCT`, `REORG_FRAG_PCT`, `REORG_MIN_CARD`),
or the post-run CARD=-1 detection query.
Claude must not: execute against a live database directly.

### Tier 2 — Online Reorganisation (online, long-running, lock impact)
*Examples: reorg_online.sh*

Claude may: edit logging, output formatting, Ansible integration.
Claude must flag for DBA review: any change to REORG clauses, INPLACE eligibility logic,
lock timeout handling, or table selection criteria.
Claude must flag for sysadmin review: any change to the Ansible playbook that removes or
weakens the job concurrency limit on the AWX template.
Claude must not: execute against a live database directly.

### Tier 3 — Offline Operations (maintenance window, approval gate required)
*Examples: reorg_offline.sh, backup_verify.sh*

Claude may: edit logging and output formatting only.
Claude must flag for DBA review: any change to operational logic whatsoever.
Claude must flag for sysadmin review: any Ansible playbook change. Offline playbooks
**must** retain the AWX approval node task. Claude must refuse to remove it and must
include it when creating any new offline playbook from scratch.
Claude must not: execute against a live database directly. Must not suggest removing
the approval gate under any framing.

